 we chose as a title forensic  pathology  from crime scene to courtroom evidence  to testimony so let's start  we have the crime scene and it's  interesting in light of  what has been going on in calamitous  catastrophic fashion in the united  states of america  in just the past few weeks  months and what is still going on  with the charvin trial who now concluded  to go to the jury next week and with  other cases  um still being handled  the shooting of someone by an officer  using a gun instead of a taser  by a another video i saw today  of a 15 year old african-american boy  looks to me putting his hands up and  being shot  i i don't want to digress completely  into  uh police related deaths although we'll  certainly be talking about that and what  we shall be discussing  encompasses all of those kinds of cases  so we talk about the crime scene this  can be  known alleged or suspected crime scene  it may not necessarily be a crime scene  you don't know that  um when you have a  case reported to you a body being found  it could be in the water the lake the  ocean  a stream uh it could be  anywhere in a house on the road um  and it may appear to be someone who has  been traumatized  there could be blood and so on but you  don't know  do you is it a homicide accident or  suicide  let me point out um  the medical examiner the coroner and  i'll use those terms and refer to those  offices  in a kind of um you know overall  sense no differentiation for purposes  of this discussion the death certificate  ultimately to be written  by the office m e or coroner  includes not only the cause of death a  due to b due to c the contributing  factors  but also includes something not found  on a death certificate written  completed by a doctor whether it's a  private practice or at a hospital  and that's the manner of death those  death certificates  don't include matter of death  decisions the m.e coroners do and there  are  five possible manners of death in  decreasing order  of frequency of occurrence you have  natural  accident suicide homicide and  undetermined um and  you can see in the recently completed  presentation in the chaven trial  obviously the prosecution is saying it's  a homicide  the defense witnesses suggesting it  might be natural  and one of them said well he might have  signed it out as undetermined  um so keep that in mind so  back to the discovery of a body  you want to be very very careful uh in  not contaminating  the scene we have long known about this  going back to uh well locard's  principal l-o-c-a-r-d a french  criminalist  of the early part of the 20th century  that anytime there is a  contact between one person and another  or between a person  and some inanimate object there will be  the transfer  of evidence of that contact low cards  principle well it's  pretty true although with gloves and  things like that maybe you can  avoid leaving evidence but um  we're talking then about things which  are  beyond those that are obvious that are  immediately evident  but trace evidence and this includes  hair  fibers fingerprints shoe prints  little pieces of anything it could be  grass it could be soil  whether it's on somebody's shoes or  clothing or whatever  so all of that has to be kept in mind  when you have a scene you don't want to  contaminate it  because these are matters which while  seemingly trivial uh perhaps  to the viewer  at the beginning may in a courtroom  um with skilled attorneys  whether they're just being obnoxious um  as they can be brutal as they can be  as skilled and competent as they  frequently are  um you never know and they'll get into  something from the very beginning  so you should keep that in mind  if possible depending upon  the seeming appearance  of the case you may need to have the  forensic pathologist  and a criminalist and a forensic  photographer  as well as the homicide detectives  present at the scene  from the time the body is discovered  and nothing is contaminated the scene is  cut  off and nobody does anything until  these people come now unlike  television this isn't going to be the  case across the board  with of all deaths and nobody has that  kind of  manpower nor is it necessary  you know it's not required but um  it should be kept in mind  obviously in more rural areas you're not  going to have  such people available and in larger  metropolitan areas  while people like that are available  they're pretty busy too  going back several decades from the  beginning  for example of the medical examiner's  office  in 1877 in new york city it was  customary  for a pathologist from the office to go  to the scene  of every case well many years ago now  they discovered that was simply not  going to be  physically possible it just wasn't going  to be  feasible from a manpower standpoint and  so  um while that is uh  optimal um in  something that you you talk about uh  pragmatically speaking  it's not going to happen but um  taking pictures by his killed  photographer is important  because photographic evidence of  what was the position of the body what  was  the spread of the blood what was the  relationship  of articles of clothing where was the  gun  in relationship to the hand trying to  see  if it's a suicide versus an accident  versus a homicide  and so on so those are things  that you want to keep in mind  there's no hurry there's no rush the  individual is dead  you do want to check for rigor mortis  body stiffening libor mortis  gravitational settling  of blood in the dependent parts of the  body  depending upon the position of the body  when the individual dies  and al gore mortis body temperature that  should be done  and so frequently it is not done because  then as hours go by  the scientific validity of those  criteria  um becomes ever more  weakened these are not unlike a novel  fixed times to the minute or even to the  hour  there's a spread and so you want to move  quickly  failure to have done so for example in  the  nicole simpson case in the  jonbenet ramsey case that was very very  bad  because it was important to try to  determine when  nicole simpson and ron goldman in the  o.j simpson matter  died it was important to know when  jonbenet ramsey  died in reconstructing everything so  aside from that though  once you've done that then there's no  big hurry  so you should not rush the crime scene  review now the body is taken  by the representatives of  the medical legal investigative office  to  that office it is further identified  and again things have to be done  in documented fashion photography  is important along the way because  questions come up then  about again the position of the clothing  position of the head and the hand and so  on  as rigor mortis begins to maybe  leave the body as the body is being  moved  and repositioned inadvertently and so on  so these are things that need to be done  with the body there finally on the table  further identification  photography collection of evidence  routinely hair head hair  a body hair a pubic hair  are collected in cases  of suspected rape sexual assault of any  kind  um oral anal  erectile and vaginal swabs with a woman  oral and rectal anal schwabs with  a male so those things are  done fingernail scrapings um  and then fingernail clippings um are  done  and then if there are other things um  that need to be collected  uh items uh observations  on the clothing before the clothing is  removed  that is done in organized and  systematic fashion again photographing  things  as you proceed so  then um when all of that is taken care  of  the forensic pathologist moves in  with his her assistance and you begin  the actual dissection of the body  customarily i and i think most forensic  pathologists  prefer to open the head first because  that allows then  um blood to flow out  and um presents a more valid picture  uh when you get into the thoracic and  abdominal  organs you um make the incision  over the scalp observe what you have  beneath the scalp and then you remove  the calvarium  the bony top of the skull  and look to see any evidence of  epidural subdural subarachnoid  hemorrhage  then again photographing as you go along  anything of an abnormal nature and even  routine pictures to show  that there is nothing that is abnormal  or atypical when the brain is taken out  of the cranial vault  then you examine the base of the skull  and  document that then you examine the brain  in most cases the brain will be examined  at the time where the brain has been  traumatized  whether by accident suicide homicide  gunshot wound blunt force trauma  it is wise in cases that may be  homicides  or cases that present a problem to not  examine the brain at that time a brain  is  as much a viscous very thick liquid  as it is a solid in the fresh state and  when it is traumatized by gunshot wounds  it will begin to fall apart if you try  to cut it  so you fix it informally you may  remember from your high school  and university classes when you work  with specimens  in your biology class whether it was a  frog or a  whatever and so on you fix it in formula  and it hardens and not to be crude but  to get this point across  you now have something that had the  substance  of a well done soft boiled egg to begin  with  that would flow and fall apart to  a substance with  a firmness of a hard-boiled egg  so you seriously section it like you  would a hard-boiled egg and making a  salad  and now you can follow through this for  example  was not done in the john f kennedy  assassination  we came to learn later on that the brain  while removed and placed in formalin  properly  and while the pathologist went back two  weeks later they never dissected the  brain  they never examined it i had my thoughts  as to why  because it would have shown exactly that  there were two shots  that hit the president in the head one  from the rear  and one from the front but that's the  subject for another day  the point is that discussion  of a highly controversial nature  continues to this day  vis-a-vis the jfk assassination largely  because  the brain was not examined you can't do  that  you have to examine the brain properly  and you do that after two weeks  other times you fix the brain  and a neuropathologist examines it when  you're looking for  neurodegenerative disease processes  alzheimer's and the various  neurotrophic diseases parkinson's  amyotrophic  lateral sclerosis lou gehrig's disease  and multiple sclerosis  and so on with special stains  that the trained forensic  neuropathologist  does after you threw with that then you  open the  front of the body with a y-shaped  incision called that  you begin in front of the shoulders and  you come down  diagonally and then at the base of the  sternum the breastbone  continue in a straight line through the  abdomen you reflect the soft tissues  and then after you've checked the  ribcage and the sternum  then you remove the sternum electric saw  cutting it off  from the rib cage and you now have  access to  all the organs and tissues in the  thoracic  and abdominal cavities and into the  pelvic cavity  you look at everything in place remember  you're photographing  anything of an abnormal nature see if  there's any  liquid that has accumulated blood or  other fluids  and then before you go further  you collect things for  toxicological analyses that includes  blood  ideally taken from the femoral artery as  opposed to blood  from the heart it's more valid in terms  of the distribution pattern  as to what was extant in that body when  the individual died  more so than blood from the heart so you  get blood  ideally from that femoral artery then  you get bile bili from the gallbladder  urine from the urinary bladder  those are the basics you may  then be acquiring a tissue also  for toxological studies depending on  what you're looking for  gastric contents many times but the  three  body fluids that are important are blood  bowel and urine blood will tell you what  was present  when the individual died and therefore  what was being played out  physiologically um that which is in the  bowel will give you some idea of what  had  already been metabolized by the liver  and stored  in the gallbladder which is what bowel  is the liver metabolizes  and stores and then urine later  after everything has gone through and  they've been excreted  by the kidneys so again referring to the  chauvin case  testimony by the defense about george  floyd  having ingested some pills or substance  after he was arrested  it's there's no consequence the total  total  ludicrous nonsense designed to confuse  the jury  because aside from the fact whether or  not he did that  is of no consequence because that which  he may have taken into his mouth  during this altercation that went on for  some minutes  would have had no meaning at all going  into the stomach  it takes 30 40 minutes even on an empty  stomach to be absorbed how different  does it make this is the kind of  nonsense that i  you know as an attorney and a medical  legal person really despise  uh i recognize what attorneys try to do  but uh that doesn't make it um  more palatable and acceptable to me  because i consider it to be  unethical and improper and  a sign of lack of some  competency and basic decency  so now after you've acquired those  specimens  you begin to remove your organs usually  most of the time organs are removed  separately sometimes  they remove we say on block en  bloc on block together but most of the  time  individually you weigh you measure  you um feel you get a sense of the  consistency  and you describe things you again  have your assistant or the photographer  depending again on the nature of the  case if it's a gunshot wound case  you want to document as you proceed in  from the gunshot wound  of entrance that you've identified into  the  body organs and tissues where did the  bullets reverse  and then you go all the way through to  the exit wound  and then sometimes you may insert a rod  to show the directionality of that  gunshot wound  just this past week we had two multiple  gunshot wound cases  for chicago and new york uh it's routine  probably daily uh we're not a rural  community  in pittsburgh and the communities for  which i do autopsies  but still a two multiple gunshot wounds  within a matter of a few days is not  that common  even in one of those homicide  centers and that's those are difficult  cases  you've got to identify and differentiate  between  entrance and exit contrary to what  people believe  sometimes it's not clear-cut as to what  is entrance and what is exit  depending upon the um the location of  the  of the weapon in relationship to the  body  and whether there's gunshot powder  residue a deposited  stippling pattern um that comes out  with the burning carbonaceous particles  within 24  18 24 inches fired from  the victim and so on so  you do all of that then you take pieces  of tissue after you've weighed and  measured  and palpated and described everything  you take what we call  representative sections you take pieces  of all of the major organs and smaller  organs  and submit them in formalin we call them  representative  sections because you want to get  something which is representative of  both pathological changes as well as  normal appearing tissues and those are  placed in formalin for microscopic  slide preparation just like the hospital  pathologist  gets the appendix or the lung tumor or  the piece of kidney  from the hospital surgeon um  he she examines it and makes a slide  and looks at it under the microscope  it's the same thing  with the forensic pathologist  so now you've got working for you  toxicological analyses uh by  a toxicologist in a separate laboratory  and you've got slides being made you may  have made some cultures  sometimes you want to see if there's an  infectious process  so you do a culture with a swab  and you get back to these slides  of the swabs you've made looking for  possible  evidence of rape sexual assault  looking for seminal fluid looking for  spermatozoa and so on um  and you're getting all of that to come  back  to you now you're getting  further information from your own  personnel  as well as the  homicide detectors and so on and you put  everything together  i want to make a very important point  um you do not  rely upon information from the police  and homicide investigator if it were  scientific testimony  you are not an adjunct of  the prosecution look at the national  academy  of sciences report in late february  of 2009 they made it clear  this most prestigious group of  scientists in the world  national academy of sciences making it  very clear  something that i talked about for  decades not that i was  the first or only person but i was so  proud and thrilled  to see that published in february of 09  that the people in the fields of  forensic science  whether the pathologist the criminalist  whoever you are  not a norm you are not an extension of  the prosecution  you are functioning as a forensic  scientist  so unlike the pathologist  in the george floyd case the hennepin  county medical examiner  who gave a diagnosis of cardiopulmonary  arrest  occurring during the subduel of the  victim  during physical restraint  uh application of physical restraints  and so on  total total prosecutorially biased  way of presenting a case first of all  cardiopulmonary arrest  is not something you see grossly or  microscopically  it means heart and lungs stop right  cardiopulmonary arrest  when you die everybody you're going to  have cardiopulmonary arrest  if there's a clinician present when  somebody goes into that kind  of clinical situation then he she can  say  cardiopulmonary arrest they see it they  hear it with a stethoscope they view it  on the electrocardiogram you're not  saying that at the autopsy table  as number number two you don't use that  information  from the police during subdue  a noun you won't even find in some  dictionaries  um we know what it means  uh enduring the uh attempt to restrain  or whatever and so on um this kind of  partiality  was demonstrated during the testimony  the medical examiner  who testified obviously with great  discomfort and displeasure  called by the prosecution so you take  all this together and then you put  together a postmortem protocol the  autopsy report  that autopsy report should contain  things  of an objective scientific nature if you  want to express  opinions then you may be consulted  by attorneys by the district attorney by  whomever  families in private cases that's a  separate  written document called a medical legal  report  when i get a consultation and i do an  autopsy  i give my autopsy report that's one  thing  and the consultation report is separate  you do not admix your subjective  interpretations with the objective  findings  i want to pound that point home  understand you're a forensic scientist  if you want to be a police officer or a  homicide detective  wonderful pursuits do that don't get it  confused  with being a forensic scientist i pound  that point home  with students any who are listening make  up your mind  you want to be a policeman you want to  be a district attorney  or you want to be a criminal defense  attorney or do you want to be a  scientist  keep that in mind so there's your final  post-warning protocol  and your medical legal consultation  report  i see now we've come to 30 minutes i  hope i have covered everything for you  from beginning uh to end from  um crime scene to courtroom um  evidence to testimony i hope  that i've made some points that will be  helpful to you  as you pursue your present and future  careers  and maybe if you are a teacher keep  these in mind  things to get across to your students  thank you very much  folks i wish you well in all of your  future endeavors  best wishes

