that discussion  of a highly controversial nature  continues to this day  vis-a-vis the jfk assassination largely  because  the brain was not examined you can't do  that  you have to examine the brain properly  and you do that after two weeks  other times you fix the brain  and a neuropathologist examines it when  you're looking for  neurodegenerative disease processes  alzheimer's and the various  neurotrophic diseases parkinson's  amyotrophic  lateral sclerosis lou gehrig's disease  and multiple sclerosis  and so on with special stains  that the trained forensic  neuropathologist  does after you threw with that then you  open the  front of the body with a y-shaped  incision called that  you begin in front of the shoulders and  you come down  diagonally and then at the base of the  sternum the breastbone  continue in a straight line through the  abdomen you reflect the soft tissues  and then after you've checked the  ribcage and the sternum  then you remove the sternum electric saw  cutting it off  from the rib cage and you now have  access to  all the organs and tissues in the  thoracic  and abdominal cavities and into the  pelvic cavity  you look at everything in place remember  you're photographing  anything of an abnormal nature see if  there's any  liquid that has accumulated blood or  other fluids  and then before you go further  you collect things for  toxicological analyses that includes  blood  ideally taken from the femoral artery as  opposed to blood  from the heart it's more valid in terms  of the distribution pattern  as to what was extant in that body when  the individual died  more so than blood from the heart so you  get blood  ideally from that femoral artery then  you get bile bili from the gallbladder  urine from the urinary bladder  those are the basics you may  then be acquiring a tissue also  for toxological studies depending on  what you're looking for  gastric contents many times but the  three  body fluids that are important are blood  bowel and urine blood will tell you what  was present  when the individual died and therefore  what was being played out  physiologically um that which is in the  bowel will give you some idea of what  had  already been metabolized by the liver  and stored  in the gallbladder which is what bowel  is the liver metabolizes  and stores and then urine later  after everything has gone through and  they've been excreted  by the kidneys so again referring to the  chauvin case  testimony by the defense about george  floyd  having ingested some pills or substance  after he was arrested  it's there's no consequence the total  total  ludicrous nonsense designed to confuse  the jury  because aside from the fact whether or  not he did that  is of no consequence because that which  he may have taken into his mouth  during this altercation that went on for  some minutes  would have had no meaning at all going  into the stomach  it takes 30 40 minutes even on an empty  stomach to be absorbed how different  does it make this is the kind of  nonsense that i  you know as an attorney and a medical  legal person really despise  uh i recognize what attorneys try to do  but uh that doesn't make it um  more palatable and acceptable to me  because i consider it to be  unethical and improper and  a sign of lack of some  competency and basic decency  so now after you've acquired those  specimens  you begin to remove your organs usually  most of the time organs are removed  separately sometimes  they remove we say on block en  bloc on block together but most of the  time  individually you weigh you measure  you um feel you get a sense of the  consistency  and you describe things you again  have your assistant or the photographer  depending again on the nature of the  case if it's a gunshot wound case  you want to document as you proceed in  from the gunshot wound  of entrance that you've identified into  the  body organs and tissues where did the  bullets reverse  and then you go all the way through to  the exit wound  and then sometimes you may insert a rod  to show the directionality of that  gunshot wound  just this past week we had two multiple  gunshot wound cases  for chicago and new york uh it's routine  probably daily uh we're not a rural  community  in pittsburgh and the communities for  which i do autopsies  but still a two multiple gunshot wounds  within a matter of a few days is not  that common  even in one of those homicide  centers and that's those are difficult  cases  you've got to identify and differentiate  between  entrance and exit contrary to what  people believe  sometimes it's not clear-cut as to what  is entrance and what is exit  depending upon the um the location of  the  of the weapon in relationship to the  body  and whether there's gunshot powder  residue a deposited  stippling pattern um that comes out  with the burning carbonaceous particles  within 24  18 24 inches fired from  the victim and so on so  you do all of that then you take pieces  of tissue after you've weighed and  measured  and palpated and described everything  you take what we call  representative sections you take pieces  of all of the major organs and smaller  organs  and submit them in formalin we call them  representative  sections because you want to get  something which is representative of  both pathological changes as well as  normal appearing tissues and those are  placed in formalin for microscopic  slide preparation just like the hospital  pathologist  gets the appendix or the lung tumor or  the piece of kidney  from the hospital surgeon um  he she examines it and makes a slide  and looks at it under the microscope  it's the same thing  with the forensic pathologist  so now you've got working for you  toxicological analyses uh by  a toxicologist in a separate laboratory  and you've got slides being made you may  have made some cultures  sometimes you want to see if there's an  infectious process  so you do a culture with a swab  and you get back to these slides  of the swabs you've made looking for  possible  evidence of rape sexual assault  looking for seminal fluid looking for  spermatozoa and so on um  and you're getting all of that to come  back  to you now you're getting  further information from your own  personnel  as well as the  homicide detectors and so on and you put  everything together  i want to make a very important point  um you do not  rely upon information from the police  and homicide investigator if it were  scientific testimony  you are not an adjunct of  the prosecution look at the national  academy  of sciences report in late february  of 2009 they made it clear  this most prestigious group of  scientists in the world  national academy of sciences making it  very clear  something that i talked about for  decades not that i was  the first or only person but i was so  proud and thrilled  to see that published in february of 09  that the people in the fields of  forensic science  whether the pathologist the criminalist  whoever you are  not a norm you are not an extension of  the prosecution  you are functioning as a forensic  scientist  so unlike the pathologist  in the george floyd case the hennepin  county medical examiner  who gave a diagnosis of cardiopulmonary  arrest  occurring during the subduel of the  victim  during physical restraint  uh application of physical restraints  and so on  total total prosecutorially biased  way of presenting a case first of all  cardiopulmonary arrest  is not something you see grossly or  microscopically  it means heart and lungs stop right  cardiopulmonary arrest  when you die everybody you're going to  have cardiopulmonary arrest  if there's a clinician present when  somebody goes into that kind  of clinical situation then he she can  say  cardiopulmonary arrest they see it they  hear it with a stethoscope they view it  on the electrocardiogram you're not  saying that at the autopsy table  as number number two you don't use that  information  from the police during subdue  a noun you won't even find in some  dictionaries  um we know what it means  uh enduring the uh attempt to restrain  or whatever and so on um this kind of  partiality  was demonstrated during the testimony  the medical examiner  who testified obviously with great  discomfort and displeasure  called by the prosecution so you take  all this together and then you put  together a postmortem protocol the  autopsy report  that autopsy report should contain  things  of an objective scientific nature if you  want to express  opinions then you may be consulted  by attorneys by the district attorney by  whomever  families in private cases that's a  separate  written document called a medical legal  report  when i get a consultation and i do an  autopsy  i give my autopsy report that's one  thing  and the consultation report is separate  you do not admix your subjective  interpretations with the objective  findings  i want to pound that point home  understand you're a forensic scientist  if you want to be a police officer or a  homicide detective  wonderful pursuits do that don't get it  confused  with being a forensic scientist i pound  that point home  with students any who are listening make  up your mind  you want to be a policeman you want to  be a district attorney  or you want to be a criminal defense  attorney or do you want to be a  scientist  keep that in mind so there's your final  post-warning protocol  and your medical legal consultation  report  i see now we've come to 30 minutes i  hope i have covered everything for you  from beginning uh to end from  um crime scene to courtroom um  evidence to testimony i hope  that i've made some points that will be  helpful to you  as you pursue your present and future  careers  and maybe if you are a teacher keep  these in mind  things to get across to your students  thank you very much  folks i wish you well in all of your  future endeavors  best wishes
 

